
This plugin controls the playback of mpris-enabled applications in this device,
commanded by its counterpart in the other device that acts as a remote control.
That means both plugins are not symmetrical.

This plugins receives and sends packages with type kdeconnect.mpris.

It keeps a list of detected players it can control via MPRIS. When it receives
a package that contains the boolean "requestPlayerList" set to true, it will
send back the list of players in an array named "playerList". If a new player is
detected or a known one disappears, it should also send this list. Note that
players are identified only by its name (its MPRIS Identity), so there can not
be two players with the same display name.

This plugins also reports the current song, extracted from MPRIS Metadata. It
should send it when it changes or when receiving a package containing a boolean
"requestNowPlaying" set to true.

The remote devices can send packages with commands to one of the players. Those
packages will contain a string "player" with the name of the player they want to
command and a string "action" with the name of an MPRIS call (like "Play",
"Next"...).

This plugin can also control the system volume. The peer device can send a
package with "requestVolume" set to true to ask for the current volume, or send
a package with "setVolume" set to an integer in the range [0,100] to change it.

macOS support is experimental and is not built by default. Configure with
`-DWITH_MACOS_MEDIAREMOTE=ON` to enable it. The backend loads Apple's private
MediaRemote framework at runtime with `dlopen`/`dlsym`; set
`KDECONNECT_DISABLE_MACOS_MEDIAREMOTE=1` to force the safe fallback path. Missing
frameworks or symbols degrade to a single safe "Now Playing" player with limited
metadata from Music/Spotify AppleScript or an optional helper named by
`KDECONNECT_MACOS_MEDIAREMOTE_HELPER` (JSON on stdout). MediaRemote is private
API: controls/metadata may be gated by macOS entitlements, can no-op after OS
updates, and may affect signing/notarization review.

Since macOS 15.4 Apple gates direct MediaRemote reads and, as of macOS 27,
command delivery for third-party processes. When the external
`media-control` helper (https://github.com/ungive/media-control, Homebrew:
`brew install media-control`) is present, the backend uses it as the preferred
generic source for metadata, artwork, player identity and playback commands,
including seek. Its path can be overridden with `KDECONNECT_MACOS_MEDIA_CONTROL`.
It names the reported player after the application that currently owns
now playing (for example "Sonora"); without it the generic "Now Playing"
player is reported. Every application that owned now playing during the
session is listed as its own player with its last known state. Inactive
entries stay controllable only when the application is scriptable
(Music, Spotify), since macOS otherwise routes third-party commands to
the application that currently owns now playing.

